<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eros</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eros was the Greek god of love and desire, in early myth a primeval force and later the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, shown as a winged youth with bow and arrows. The Boeotian city of Thespiae was his chief cult centre, with a famous statue by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praxiteles">Praxiteles</a> and a festival, the Erotidia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew him as Cupid or Amor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eros.jpg" alt="Eros" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Hermoso Cuesta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eros_Farnese_N%C3%A1poles_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Eros is the ancient Greek god of love and sex, whose name means desire. In the earliest sources, the cosmogonies, the earliest philosophers and texts on the mystery religions, he is one of the primordial gods involved in the coming into being of the cosmos. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony, from about 700 BCE, he is the fourth god to exist, after Chaos, Gaia and Tartarus, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmenides">Parmenides</a> makes him the first of all. In The Birds, a comedy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> of 414 BCE, he springs from an egg laid by Night. In later sources he is the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> made him her son by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Eros is usually shown as a handsome young man, though sometimes as a mischievous boy in the company of his mother, and he is winged and carries a bow and arrows with which he makes mortals and gods fall in love. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> gives him two kinds of arrow, golden ones that bring love and lead ones that bring aversion, as in the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and Daphne. His role in myth is mostly to prompt love, and he has little mythology of his own beyond the story of Eros and Psyche, told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a>'s Latin novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-golden-ass">The Golden Ass</a>, in which Psyche, the deified human soul, wins him after tasks set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> and is made immortal by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>.</p><p>A cult of Eros existed in pre-classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> but was much less important than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> he shared a popular cult with her, and the fourth day of every month was sacred to him. In late antiquity he was worshipped by a fertility cult in Thespiae, where the Thespians held the Erotidia, festivals of Eros. The Samians consecrated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> to him, and the Lacedaemonians and Cretans sacrificed to him before battle, thinking victory depended on the friendship of those who stand side by side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Later satirical poets showed Eros as a blindfolded child, the forerunner of the chubby Renaissance Cupid. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called him Cupid or Amor. In art he appears as a putto, an image that seems to have influenced the figure of the cherub in Christian art of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and Renaissance, and the later Cupid became a major symbol of Valentine's Day. The ancient motif of Eros stung by bees, found in the Anacreontea and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a>, was retold many times in antiquity and the Renaissance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thespiae's chief cult honoured <b>Eros</b>, whose statue by Praxiteles later drew visitors, and it held the Mouseia festival for the Muses.</blockquote><a href="/state/thespiae">Thespiae</a></li></ul></section>
